  like.

  - [1mVendor-neutral HTTP sink[0m — logger POSTs a JSON array of records to a configurable URL with an optional auth header.
    Works as-is with Better Stack/Logtail, Datadog's HTTP intake, Grafana Loki, or anything you run yourself. Buys you a
    free switch of destination later — it's a config value, not a rewrite. Costs you vendor niceties: no automatic
    stack-trace symbolication, no session replay, no pre-built dashboards. Cheap to change.
  - [1mSentry via CDN ESM import[0m — [38;5;153mimport[39m the Sentry browser bundle from their CDN. Buys real error tracking: stack traces,
    breadcrumbs, release tracking, grouping. Costs a third-party script executing on your login page, a second
    different SDK for the Node side, and an outbound dependency on a CDN at page load. Expensive to unwind — you'd be
    coding against their API, not yours.
  - [1mDefer the destination, build the seam[0m — logger emits to console plus a pluggable sink interface, with the HTTP sink
    written but pointed at a URL you set when you've chosen. Buys you the whole structured layer now and the vendor
    decision later, with nothing wasted. Costs you: logs aren't actually leaving anywhere until you set that URL, so it
    doesn't yet answer "debug production issues."

  [1mMy recommendation: the vendor-neutral HTTP sink.[0m With no build step, you get most of the value of a hosted collector
  and keep the escape hatch. Option 3 is really option 1 with the URL left blank, so if you don't have a destination
  picked, say so and I'll write it that way — the design is the same either way.
